---
import Scene from '../Scene.astro';
import Tex from '../Tex.astro';
import Slider from '../Slider.astro';
import Readout from '../Readout.astro';
---
<Scene id="secant" label="The curve y equals x cubed with a fixed point P at (2, 8), a second point Q that slides toward P, the secant line through them, and the tangent line the secants approach, slope 12.">
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Pick a point">
    <p>Take the curve <Tex t="y = \cA{x^3}"/> and the point <Tex t="P = (2, 8)"/>. How steep is the curve <em>at</em> <Tex t="P"/>?</p>
    <p>A slope is rise over run, and that needs two points. With only one point there is nothing to divide.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Add a second point">
    <p>So cheat a little. Put a second point <Tex t="Q"/> a distance <Tex t="h"/> to the right, and draw the <span class="k-c3">secant line</span> through <Tex t="P"/> and <Tex t="Q"/>.</p>
    <Tex display t="\text{slope}_{PQ} = \frac{f(2+h) - f(2)}{h}"/>
    <p class="aside">With <Tex t="h = 1"/>: <Tex t="(27 - 8)/1 = 19"/>. That is too steep, because the curve bends upward between <Tex t="P"/> and <Tex t="Q"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Slide Q toward P">
    <p>Now slide <Tex t="Q"/> toward <Tex t="P"/>. The run shrinks, and so does the rise, but their <em>ratio</em> does not go to zero. It settles on a value.</p>
    <p class="aside">The table: <Tex t="h=0.001"/> gives <Tex t="12.006001"/>, and <Tex t="h=0.0001"/> gives <Tex t="12.00060"/>. The guess is <Tex t="12"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="The tangent">
    <p>The value it settles on is the slope of the <span class="k-c2">tangent line</span>, the line that just grazes the curve at <Tex t="P"/>. That limit is the <strong>derivative</strong>:</p>
    <Tex display t="\cB{f'(2)} = \lim_{h\to 0}\frac{f(2+h)-f(2)}{h} = 12"/>
    <p class="aside">Tangent line: <Tex t="y - 8 = 12(x-2)"/>, or <Tex t="y = 12x - 16"/>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="beat" data-beat="Every point">
    <p>Drag <Tex t="P"/> along the curve. Every point has its own tangent slope, and for <Tex t="x^3"/> it is always <Tex t="3a^2"/>. Average velocity over a short interval works the same way: it approaches the instantaneous velocity.</p>
  </div>
  <Tex slot="takeaway" display t="\cB{f'(a)} = \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{\cA{f(a+h)} - \cA{f(a)}}{\cC{h}}"/>
  <Fragment slot="controls">
    <Slider name="h" label="Gap h" min={-3} max={0.25} step={0.01} value={0} color="c3" digits={4}/>
    <Readout name="secant" label="Secant slope" color="c3"/>
    <Readout name="tangent" label="Tangent slope" color="c2"/>
  </Fragment>
</Scene>
